WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.400
-It's a party. -It feels good.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:03.734
It feels good when you -- -Yeah, it's a party.

00:00:03.734 --> 00:00:05.534
-Thank you. Welcome back. And thank you for stopping by.

00:00:05.534 --> 00:00:07.000
And thank you for -- -Thanks for having me.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.167
-Thanks for playing yet again. We did another fun --

00:00:09.167 --> 00:00:10.701
-We have played so much.

00:00:10.701 --> 00:00:12.234
-We've had so many fun bits.

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:14.634
-You know, the first crazy skit I did,

00:00:14.634 --> 00:00:16.901
if you all recall, was --

00:00:16.901 --> 00:00:18.467
-In the White -- -In the White House.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:20.734
And we did a potato-sack race in the East Room.

00:00:20.734 --> 00:00:22.601
[ Laughter ] -That's right. We did.

00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:25.467
You were the first silly I had. [ Laughter ]

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:26.767
-I like being the first silly. I love that.

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:30.000
-So, thank you for that. -I gotta say, I love having you.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:31.334
I love talking to you, obviously.

00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.634
But to see people's reaction to you next to you,

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:38.000
it makes me feel so good. People love you.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.367
I mean, we all love you, but we really --

00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:43.234
[ Cheers and applause ] -Aww, you guys...

00:00:43.234 --> 00:00:44.701
-[ Speaking indistinctly ]

00:00:44.701 --> 00:00:47.234
-Look, I don't take it for granted.

00:00:47.234 --> 00:00:49.968
Look, we don't get out much. -Oh, yeah.

00:00:49.968 --> 00:00:54.267
-So it's just nice to hear what it all meant to people,

00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.534
particularly young people.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:58.067
So I don't take any of that for granted.

00:00:58.067 --> 00:01:01.033
-Do you ever get starstruck anymore, or...?

00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:03.767
-Oh. Pfft! Questlove.

00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:05.667
-Oh, come on. Oh, come on.

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:09.334
-Hi, hi! [ Both laugh ]

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:11.234
Absolutely I get starstruck. -You do?

00:01:11.234 --> 00:01:14.434
-Well, maybe not exactly right now, in this moment,

00:01:14.434 --> 00:01:16.901
but in the course of my life... -What?

00:01:16.901 --> 00:01:19.467
-I mean, you just imagine the people that we met.

00:01:19.467 --> 00:01:22.334
Stevie? You know, when I first met Stevie Wonder?

00:01:22.334 --> 00:01:23.901
-Yes, of course. -I mean, I was just like,

00:01:23.901 --> 00:01:26.601
"Stevie!" I fangirled out.

00:01:26.601 --> 00:01:29.267
-You have to, yeah. -You know, Prince, I mean...

00:01:29.267 --> 00:01:30.834
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:30.834 --> 00:01:33.434
Prince played at the White House

00:01:33.434 --> 00:01:35.767
months before he passed, actually.

00:01:35.767 --> 00:01:36.767
And just -- I know. It was just --

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:40.734
-Isn't he great, the way that Prince was,

00:01:40.734 --> 00:01:42.767
also, as a person?

00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:45.434
I've had conversations with your husband about this, too,

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:47.667
that everyone is kind of who they should be.

00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:50.334
-Yeah. -Prince is a magical --

00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:52.901
-He appears. -Yes!

00:01:52.901 --> 00:01:54.868
Yes, he kind of -- Like, a puff of smoke.

00:01:54.868 --> 00:01:56.267
He just kind of shows up.

00:01:56.267 --> 00:01:57.834
-He's in the Blue Room, standing in front

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.501
of President Obama, and he's just like, "Yo."

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:01.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:01.934 --> 00:02:03.634
-He's just cool. -Still right here.

00:02:03.634 --> 00:02:05.167
-Yeah, exactly. -So, yes,

00:02:05.167 --> 00:02:06.501
I fangirl out all the time.

00:02:06.501 --> 00:02:08.934
-Congratulations on "The Light We Carry."

00:02:08.934 --> 00:02:14.267
It's another New York Times best seller.

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:17.434
And the book -- You dedicate the book to your parents.

00:02:17.434 --> 00:02:20.367
-Oh, my God. -Which watch I do love.

00:02:20.367 --> 00:02:22.067
You've talked about the influence

00:02:22.067 --> 00:02:24.033
that your father had on you,

00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.033
but also as a whole chapter here about your mom, Marion.

00:02:28.033 --> 00:02:30.367
-Yes. -She deserved a chapter.

00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:32.567
She's -- She's -- You know, my parents

00:02:32.567 --> 00:02:35.067
are where I get any wisdom that I have.

00:02:35.067 --> 00:02:39.434
And the fact that she allowed me to even say her name in the book

00:02:39.434 --> 00:02:42.167
is a big deal because Marian Robinson

00:02:42.167 --> 00:02:44.834
isn't impressed with anything at any time.

00:02:44.834 --> 00:02:46.667
So I had to ask permission. I was like, "Mom --"

00:02:46.667 --> 00:02:49.000
We were trying to get her to write a book.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:50.901
She's like, "Nobody wants to hear what I have to say."

00:02:50.901 --> 00:02:52.200
I was like, "No, mom, everybody..."

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.033
-Everybody wants to hear, yeah. -"...wants to hear

00:02:54.033 --> 00:02:56.300
what you have to say." -She does have some great

00:02:56.300 --> 00:02:58.767
tried and true parenting in the book.

00:02:58.767 --> 00:03:02.901
Number five is "Come Home. We will always like you here."

00:03:02.901 --> 00:03:04.834
-Yeah. Yeah. [ Laughter ]

00:03:04.834 --> 00:03:06.868
That is true. -I mean, let me tell you

00:03:06.868 --> 00:03:08.534
how grounding that is.

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.801
And for parents, it's important to remember.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:14.200
Because she said that because she knew

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:16.734
that, as kids of color, as women,

00:03:16.734 --> 00:03:19.801
as a woman, young woman, young Black woman in the world,

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:21.801
that I would be confronted with a lot of people

00:03:21.801 --> 00:03:25.501
who would set my bar low for me.

00:03:25.501 --> 00:03:30.868
You know, she knew we were going to hear racial insults.

00:03:30.868 --> 00:03:33.267
She knew what we were going to confront.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:36.434
And she never wanted us to get our self-worth

00:03:36.434 --> 00:03:39.767
from outside the home because we couldn't count on it.

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:43.834
So that maxim is "Don't sweat the small stuff."

00:03:43.834 --> 00:03:47.000
Don't lose it because a teacher doesn't like you,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:49.400
because somebody called you a bad word.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.601
Don't let your self-esteem be based on that.

00:03:52.601 --> 00:03:55.033
You know, get your self-worth at home.

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:59.200
And so that's a powerful thing for all parents to remember.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:02.000
But it's an important thing for adults to remember

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.868
as we deal with young people, that our words to them matter.

00:04:05.868 --> 00:04:07.000
You know? -Yeah.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.200
-What we say, how we treat them,

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:13.167
it has an impact, because some kids don't have a home

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:15.567
to go to to feel that love.

00:04:15.567 --> 00:04:18.767
So I think about that every time I interact with a kid

00:04:18.767 --> 00:04:20.100
that my interaction with them

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:23.567
may be the time they feel safe and they feel at home.

00:04:23.567 --> 00:04:26.501
So I want everybody to think about that.

00:04:26.501 --> 00:04:28.167
-That goes far. -So I got that at home.

00:04:28.167 --> 00:04:30.567
[ Cheers and applause ] -I love that.

00:04:30.567 --> 00:04:33.000
How, uh...

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.868
How are the kids?

00:04:34.868 --> 00:04:36.934
-Kids are good. Those two. -Sasha and Malia.

00:04:36.934 --> 00:04:38.400
-They're not kids. -Are they still --

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.868
-They are grown -- -They're 7 years old?

00:04:39.868 --> 00:04:41.734
How old are they? [ Laughter ]

00:04:41.734 --> 00:04:42.834
-That's how the world sees them.

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:45.501
Twent-- They'll be 25 and 22.

00:04:45.501 --> 00:04:48.234
-When did that happen? -Yeah. You all are old.

00:04:48.234 --> 00:04:50.167
[ Laughter ] When did that happen?

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:52.767
-I know. Before our very eyes.

00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:56.601
All I can say is, no more babies, no more tuition.

00:04:56.601 --> 00:04:58.434
[ Laughter ] Bye.

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:01.033
It's like, "Here's your life." -"There you go."

00:05:01.033 --> 00:05:02.267
-"Good luck." -Yeah, "Let me know

00:05:02.267 --> 00:05:03.400
how it works out." Yeah.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:05.267
But now they're living together, right?

00:05:05.267 --> 00:05:07.667
They're roommates? -Right now they are, yep, yeah.

00:05:07.667 --> 00:05:10.067
-Do you visit them? -We visited them once.

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:13.934
-Okay. -When we're your parents,

00:05:13.934 --> 00:05:16.167
you don't really want us around that much, you know?

00:05:16.167 --> 00:05:17.634
[ Laughter ] -Really?

00:05:17.634 --> 00:05:20.434
-So -- But we did get invited over for cocktails

00:05:20.434 --> 00:05:22.701
before dinner because we had to take them out to dinner,

00:05:22.701 --> 00:05:24.234
and they invited us over for cocktails.

00:05:24.234 --> 00:05:25.400
-No, no, no. -Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.567
-No, you don't drink a cocktail. -Oh. Pfft!

00:05:28.567 --> 00:05:30.734
Not only did they drink it, but they made one.

00:05:30.734 --> 00:05:33.534
-No! -That was a bad martini.

00:05:33.534 --> 00:05:34.834
[ Laughter ]

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.200
It was very weak, in a tumbling glass.

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:39.734
[ Laughter ]

00:05:39.734 --> 00:05:41.100
You know, they stumbled through --

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.000
It was mostly vermouth and ice. [ Laughter ]

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.267
-I still do the same thing.

00:05:45.267 --> 00:05:46.701
-Well, maybe it wasn't even vermouth,

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.734
because I don't even think that they knew vermouth

00:05:48.734 --> 00:05:50.100
was in a martini. -Yeah, that's exactly it.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:51.501
But they're trying to impress you with a martini.

00:05:51.501 --> 00:05:53.033
-Oh, yeah. Oh, yeah, yeah.

00:05:53.033 --> 00:05:55.634
And a charcuterie tray. -Ooh!

00:05:55.634 --> 00:05:58.100
-Which they complained about the price of cheese.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.334
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.334 --> 00:06:00.667
-That's good. -Malia's like,

00:06:00.667 --> 00:06:02.400
"Do you know how much I paid for that cheese?

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.968
[ Laughter ] And I was like, "Yeah, girl,

00:06:03.968 --> 00:06:06.334
you eat my cheese all the time." -Exactly.

00:06:06.334 --> 00:06:08.300
-Now you -- -Never worried about the price

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.300
when you eat my charcuterie tray.

00:06:10.300 --> 00:06:12.234
It's like... -You've always said

00:06:12.234 --> 00:06:13.868
when we've talked about the kids

00:06:13.868 --> 00:06:15.567
that you wanted to give them the most normal childhood

00:06:15.567 --> 00:06:17.300
that you possibly can while you're living

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:19.701
in the White House, which has got to be tough.

00:06:19.701 --> 00:06:23.033
But I remember when you were saying even Barack

00:06:23.033 --> 00:06:24.934
coached their basketball team? -Yes.

00:06:24.934 --> 00:06:27.367
Yeah, he was -- he was dad in the White House.

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:28.934
-How did you sneak out and do that, though?

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:31.834
How is that possible? -You know, it's amazing.

00:06:31.834 --> 00:06:34.267
You know, and he wasn't assigned to be the coach.

00:06:34.267 --> 00:06:37.734
This was fourth grade rec league for little girls.

00:06:37.734 --> 00:06:40.634
Right? Sasha was playing, and he and Reggie Love,

00:06:40.634 --> 00:06:41.734
who was his aide at the time,

00:06:41.734 --> 00:06:43.601
who was a Division I player for Duke,

00:06:43.601 --> 00:06:47.167
they would sit and watch these girls not have their shoes tied,

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:48.767
they weren't running plays,

00:06:48.767 --> 00:06:51.167
because it was a parent coached league, right?

00:06:51.167 --> 00:06:52.734
-Yeah. Also, they're in fourth grade.

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:54.000
-They're in fourth grade,

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:56.634
but Barack Obama felt that they could do better.

00:06:56.634 --> 00:06:58.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:58.534 --> 00:07:02.100
So each game, he would slide closer down to the players bench

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:04.834
and whisper to the parent coaches who I'm sure were like,

00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:08.767
"Oh, my God, it's the President telling me to run a play."

00:07:08.767 --> 00:07:10.167
Eventually, he took over.

00:07:10.167 --> 00:07:14.334
He had them practicing, he taught them plays.

00:07:14.334 --> 00:07:16.267
And the funny thing is, like,

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:17.667
it wasn't only Sasha on the team,

00:07:17.667 --> 00:07:20.934
but it was Maisy Biden, Joe's youngest granddaughter.

00:07:20.934 --> 00:07:22.033
They're very good friends.

00:07:22.033 --> 00:07:24.334
So imagine it, on any given Sunday,

00:07:24.334 --> 00:07:27.267
in a rec gym in Chevy Chase --

00:07:27.267 --> 00:07:29.734
'Cause we all came out, right? -Yeah.

00:07:29.734 --> 00:07:31.901
-You're playing against the Vipers.

00:07:31.901 --> 00:07:34.000
That was their name. They were the Vipers.

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:35.701
-Yeah. -It would be the President,

00:07:35.701 --> 00:07:39.067
the Vice President, the first, second lady,

00:07:39.067 --> 00:07:42.367
all the kids and all of our details.

00:07:42.367 --> 00:07:44.200
[ Laughter ]

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:46.067
So you imagine being the poor kids

00:07:46.067 --> 00:07:48.033
on the other team... [ Laughter ]

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:50.067
...with Joe yelling, "Maisy, pass the ball!"

00:07:50.067 --> 00:07:51.801
[ Laughter ] "Box out!"

00:07:51.801 --> 00:07:54.200
You know, at the time, I would have to tell them, "Chill out."

00:07:54.200 --> 00:07:56.167
"You know, this is -- these kids --

00:07:56.167 --> 00:07:57.667
I think that little girl is crying

00:07:57.667 --> 00:07:59.133
'cause the President's yelling at them."

00:07:59.133 --> 00:08:00.367
[ Laughter ] -How did they end up doing?

00:08:00.367 --> 00:08:01.734
-They won the championship.

00:08:01.734 --> 00:08:03.434
-Come on! That's exactly right!

00:08:03.434 --> 00:08:05.033
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:05.033 --> 00:08:06.400
-♪ The Vipers ♪

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:09.100
-Guys, more with Michelle Obama when we come back.

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:11.300
More "Tonight Show." Come on back, everybody.

